<article class="shell-article">
  <nav class="shell-breadcrumb"><a href="/hc/en-us">Help Center</a> / Green gas for the home</nav>

  <p>When you cook on gas or heat your house with a gas boiler, a steadily growing share of the gas comes from Danish biogas plants. Biomethane has exactly the same quality as fossil natural gas and is fed into the same gas grid – so you don't need to change anything in your home to use it.</p>

  <div class="shell-callout">
    <strong>No new installations:</strong> Your gas boiler, gas stove and pipes work exactly as before. The difference is that the gas is produced from waste in Denmark instead of being pumped up from underground.
  </div>

  <h2>What does it mean for the climate?</h2>
  <p>Biomethane is CO₂-neutral because it is part of nature's own cycle. The more green gas that is fed into the grid, the lower the overall climate footprint from heating and cooking in Denmark becomes – without requiring anything of the individual household.</p>

  <h2>Where does the gas come from?</h2>
  <p>The gas is produced locally at biogas plants from manure, food scraps and residual products from agriculture and industry. Read more about the process in the article <a href="/hc/en-us/p/saadan-produceres-biogas">How biogas is produced</a>.</p>

  <h2>A flexible complement to electricity</h2>
  <p>Green gas can be stored and used when demand is highest – for example on cold winter days when the electricity system is under pressure. That is why biomethane plays an important role as a flexible complement to solar and wind in the Danish energy system.</p>

